<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Bosnia and Herzegovina (Austro-Hungarian)</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1918 CE · capital Sarajevo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Former Ottoman province occupied and then annexed by Austria-Hungary, whose rule modernised Sarajevo and ended with the shots that started the First World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> from 1875 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> a mandate to occupy and administer Bosnia and Herzegovina, which remained nominally Ottoman. Austro-Hungarian troops crushed fierce Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a> resistance in the summer of 1878.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> would let the other have it, the province was run jointly through the common finance ministry, notably by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-kallay">Benjamin Kállay</a> from 1882 to 1903, who promoted a shared Bosnian identity. Railways, mines, forestry and schools developed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> gained European-style buildings such as the City Hall. The Ottoman landholding system with its Christian serfs was largely kept.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> annexed the territory in 1908, provoking the Bosnian Crisis, and granted a diet in 1910. On 28 June 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a> assassinated Archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>. In 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bosnia-and-herzegovina-austro-hungarian.jpg" alt="Bosnia and Herzegovina (Austro-Hungarian)" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Smooth_O · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bosnia_(1908%E2%80%931918).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sarajevo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Franz Joseph I, Benjamin Kállay</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Orthodox and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbo-Croatian, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Condominium of Bosnia and Herzegovina, Austro-Hungarian Bosnia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=bosnia-and-herzegovina-austro-hungarian" aria-label="Bosnia and Herzegovina (Austro-Hungarian) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5122 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bosnia and Herzegovina (Austro-Hungarian) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5442 880L5709 970M5442 880L5380 747M5442 880L5378 819"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5380 747h0M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5442 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5442 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sarajevo. <a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=bosnia-and-herzegovina-austro-hungarian">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
